The Hidden Advantage of Mask Resampling:A Theory of Masked Autoencoders Skip to main content Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: Why HTML? Report Issue Back to Abstract Download PDF Contents Reading mode Colour theme License: CC BY 4.0 arXiv:2610.01578v1 [stat.ML] 01 Oct 2026 The Hidden Advantage of Mask Resampling: A Theory of Masked Autoencoders Jorge Medina Moreira † † thanks: Corresponding author. jorge.medinamoreira@epfl.ch Lorenzo Bardone Lenka Zdeborová Affiliation: Statistical Physics of Computation Laboratory, Affiliation: École Polytechnique Fédérale de Lausanne (EPFL), CH-1015 Lausanne, Switzerland Abstract Why can masked prediction learn useful representations that unmasked reconstruction misses? We study this question in a high-dimensional model of a masked autoencoder (MAE) trained on data with shared latent structure and heterogeneous noise. We prove that masked linear reconstruction can recover the latent feature at linear sample complexity in regimes where unmasked linear reconstruction, equivalent to PCA, fails. The analysis also quantifies the statistical advantage of mask resampling, an established ingredient of masked pretraining. By introducing a fixed collection of K K masks per sample, we characterize its effect on feature recovery and downstream performance, identifying regimes where greater mask diversity lowers sample complexity. Guided by this prediction, we find that random cropping and flipping in standard image-training pipelines can obscure the advantage of mask resampling by renewing the prediction task even when the patch mask is fixed. Removing these transformations reveals a downstream advantage for dynamic over static masking in CNN autoencoders and vision transformers. A complementary BERT pilot finds benefits from greater mask diversity on downstream language tasks. Our results separate the benefit of the masked prediction objective from that of mask diversity, and show how a tractable theory can guide experiments that uncover advantages hidden by standard training practices. Code available at: https://github.com/SPOC-group/mask-resampling 1 Introduction Why can predicting missing parts of an input produce more useful representations than reconstructing the entire input? Building on denoising autoencoders [ 52 ] , Pathak et al. [46] predicted missing image regions from visible context and obtained more transferable representations than a standard autoencoder trained without input masking, a paradigm that now underlies masked language and image modeling [ 23 , 27 , 10 , 56 ] . Masked learning therefore couples two ingredients: a prediction objective that specifies what structure is rewarded, and an augmentation procedure that controls how many distinct prediction tasks a given sample ever presents. Despite its empirical success, our theoretical understanding of how these ingredients shape learned representations remains incomplete. We develop a high-dimensional theory that separates their effects and connects them to latent-feature recovery and downstream performance. Motivated by the observation that unmasked, i.e., the standard autoencoder, reconstruction 2 2 2 By unmasked reconstruction, we mean the standard autoencoder objective that observes the entire input and minimizes squared reconstruction error over all coordinates. can favor high-variance nuisance directions over downstream-relevant structure [ 9 ] , we consider a data model with a shared latent direction and heterogeneous coordinate-wise noise. The latent direction carries the downstream signal, while heterogeneity allows nuisance variance to dominate the leading principal component. Masking changes this competition by requiring hidden coordinates to be predicted from the visible ones, rather than rewarded for their own variance. We prove that a linear masked autoencoder recovers the latent direction with n = O ⁡ ( d ) n=O(d) samples where unmasked linear reconstruction , equivalent to PCA, fails ( Sec. 3 ). Figure 1: Isolating mask resampling reveals its contribution to representation learning. Downstream accuracy versus masking ratio ρ ho for dynamic masking (blue), static masking (yellow), and unmasked reconstruction (red). (a) Shallow autoencoder on spiked model ( Sec. 2 ); curves are theory and markers are simulations. (b) CNN autoencoder on CIFAR-10 (test top-1). (c) ViT-Base on ImageNet-100 (validation top-5). In (b,c), blue and yellow curves use no stochastic cropping or flipping. The purple reference includes cropping and flipping with dynamic mask resampling at ρ = 0.625 ho=0.625 (b) and ρ = 0.75 ho=0.75 (c) . At suitable masking ratios, resampling alone reaches comparable accuracy. Bars and shading show ± 1 \pm 1 standard deviation (SD). See App. H for details. Analyzing this model clearly reveals a second notable effect: the number of distinct masks available per sample controls how many underlying training samples are needed for latent-feature recovery. We distinguish static masking , where each sample is assigned a single random mask that is reused throughout training, from dynamic masking , commonly used in practice [ 27 , 38 ] , in which the mask is independently resampled each time a sample is used during pretraining. More generally, assigning each sample K K independent masks and reusing them thereafter interpolates between these two regimes at fixed number of underlying samples. This allows us to isolate mask diversity from data diversity. Our analysis predicts that increasing K K lowers the recovery threshold, with corresponding improvements in downstream performance in the settings studied. This prediction prompted us to revisit how the contribution of mask diversity is assessed empirically. In language modeling, dynamic masking has been reported to provide only modest gains over reusing a fixed collection of masks [ 38 ] , but their static baseline already uses K = 10 K=10 pre-generated masks per sequence rather than a single fixed mask. Data2Vec 2.0 shows substantial improvements in image classification accuracy by increasing the number of masked views per sample within each training update [ 5 ] . Furthermore, in vision, self-supervised image learning commonly uses random cropping, flipping, and color transformations to generate changing views of the same sample [ 18 , 50 ] . In MAE specifically, standard pretraining combines random cropping and flipping with mask resampling [ 27 ] . Under a newly drawn crop, for example, the same patch mask can hide different content from the original image. We therefore hypothesize that stochastic image augmentation adds another source of prediction-task diversity, obscuring the effect of resampling the mask itself. Guided by this hypothesis, we compare static and dynamic masking with and without stochastic cropping and flipping . Without these transformations, dynamic masking improves downstream accuracy in CNN autoencoders on CIFAR-10 and ViT-Base on ImageNet-100 ( Fig. 1 (b,c)). In the ViT experiments, reintroducing cropping and flipping substantially narrows the observed static–dynamic gap, mainly because static masking improves ( Fig. 5 ). Finite- K K experiments further show progressive improvements as each image is exposed to more masks ( Fig. 4 ). A complementary BERT-Medium pilot finds improvements with increasing mask diversity on GLUE tasks, extending the qualitative evidence beyond vision ( Sec. 5 ). Together, these results distinguish two benefits of masked representation learning: the prediction objective can favor useful correlations over nuisance variance, while renewing the masks can lower the sample complexity required to learn those correlations. By identifying this distinction, the theory motivates a controlled test of its relevance beyond the solvable model. The main contributions of this paper are the following: • Under suitable heterogeneous noise, we prove that a linear masked autoencoder recovers the latent direction for all sufficiently large finite sample complexity α = n / d \alpha=n/d , whereas unmasked linear reconstruction (PCA) fails for every fixed α > 0 \alpha>0 . Replica calculations complement this guarantee by predicting the recovery threshold ( Sec. 3 ). • We formulate a tractable high-dimensional model of a two-stage learning protocol: self-supervised learning (SSL) through masked reconstruction, followed by supervised linear probing with a frozen encoder ( Sec. 2 ). For linear and nonlinear autoencoders, we derive replica predictions for feature recovery and downstream accuracy as functions of the masking ratio and mask diversity, showing the benefit of resampling the masks ( Sec. 4 ). • Guided by our theoretical predictions, we test the role of mask diversity in vision and language. Dynamic masking improves downstream performance without stochastic cropping and flipping; adding these transformations substantially narrows the observed static–dynamic gap in the ViT setting, consistent with overlapping sources of prediction-task diversity. A BERT-Medium pilot provides complementary evidence for the benefit of mask diversity in language modeling ( Sec. 5 ). Further related work We outline the broad lines of related work here; App. A provides an expanded comparison with the closest references and clarifies the differences in setting, analytical scope, and contribution. Statistical-physics theory of neural networks. Our replica analysis builds on high-dimensional empirical risk minimization [ 4 , 40 , 21 ] , shallow-autoencoder dynamics [ 47 ] , and denoising autoencoders [ 20 ] . Conde Mendes et al. [19] prove an advantage of nonlinear autoencoders over PCA. Heterogeneous noise has also been studied in supervised learning [ 3 , 2 ] . Theory of masked learning. Existing work connects masked autoencoding to contrastive learning and downstream guarantees [ 60 ] , and establishes latent identifiability in hierarchical models [ 33 ] . Bisulco et al. [13] characterize linear MAEs with full reconstruction from masked inputs, including visible coordinates rather than only hidden targets. Their analysis relates patch size and masking ratio to the spatial correlations learned. Dong et al. [24] analyze decoupled coordinate predictors through rigorous ridgeless-regression risk formulas, accounting for both corrupted covariates and the samples supplying a selected prediction target. Single-coordinate prediction also connects to factored attention [ 48 ] . Wortsman Zurich et al. [55] characterize a spiked-model recovery transition in the joint matrix of single-coordinate ridge predictors and establish prediction-risk advantages over PCA in structured covariance settings. Cabannes et al. [16] distinguish sample variability from augmentation variability in SSL generalization bounds; additional views can reduce the latter without removing the former. Spiked models and heteroskedastic noise. Our linear baseline connects to spiked covariance models [ 31 ] and PCA recovery transitions [ 8 , 12 ] . Hong et al. [28] characterize PCA under heteroskedastic noise. Spectral methods addressing heteroskedasticity include weighted PCA [ 29 ] , diagonal correction through HeteroPCA [ 59 ] , and optimal estimators for doubly heteroskedastic noise [ 61 ] . We study how masking changes the reconstruction objective to promote recovery, without supplying the coordinate variances or their distribution. 2 A high-dimensional model of masked learning Data and noise heterogeneity. Our goal is to compare prediction objectives and masking protocols for a data distribution that enables sharp high-dimensional analysis. Inspired by insights into the advantage of masking from [ 9 ] and by work on unsupervised learning in high dimensions [ 31 , 22 , 37 ] , we consider a data model that separates a shared informative feature in the form of a spike from coordinate-specific nuisance fluctuations. Concretely, we consider n n samples with d d coordinates generated as 